# 4. THE MODULAR ROOM STANDARD — CRITICAL

The room must be designed as ONE independent and reusable world block.

It will eventually become part of a large connected AI Office building.

Do not treat the room as a permanently isolated diorama.

## Coordinate system

Use Three.js coordinates:

- X = East / West.
- Y = Vertical.
- Z = North / South.

Use Y-up.

Use one consistent world-unit scale for all future rooms.

### Proposed base module

PM Room footprint:

8 × 8 world units.

- Width: 8 units along X.
- Depth: 8 units along Z.
- Floor elevation: Y = 0.
- Interior wall height: approximately 3.2 units.
- Primary grid cell: 1 world unit.
- Floor thickness: approximately 0.16 units.

Use the southwest floor corner as the logical module origin:

X = 0
Y = 0
Z = 0

Room bounds:

X = 0 to 8
Z = 0 to 8

All permanent room geometry must be placed relative to this consistent origin.

Do not arbitrarily center room geometry at the world origin if doing so makes future room placement inconsistent.

The camera may target the module center independently.

### Grid standards

- Use a clearly defined tile grid.
- Position walls and openings precisely.
- Furniture may use sub-grid offsets.
- Keep floor and wall dimensions deterministic.
- Avoid objects extending beyond room boundaries unless explicitly designated as a connector.
- Ensure independently exported rooms can be positioned beside each other without unexpected overlaps.

### Connection standard

The room must have reserved modular connector locations for future adjacent rooms.

Provide:

Connector A — EAST
- At X = 8, centered around Z = 4.
- 2 world units of passage width.

Connector B — SOUTH
- At Z = 0, centered around X = 4.
- 2 world units of passage width.

Use precise connector coordinates and orientations.

Reserve these areas from obstructing furniture.

Connectors represent future doorways or shared passage openings.

They should not produce strange permanently open wall gaps when the room is viewed alone; use removable compatible wall or doorway sections.

### Module compatibility

Future rooms must use the SAME:

- World-unit definition.
- Floor elevations.
- Wall alignment.
- Grid cell size.
- Doorway width.
- Connector conventions.
- Coordinate-axis conventions.
- Material scale.
- Texture pixel density.

The first PM Room defines the baseline for subsequent modules.

